:figcap.Milestones at a glance
:efig.
:p.The current kit is :hp2.build 16:ehp2. of 14 October 2026, in 18
parts. It goes over a fresh VM/370 CE V1 R1.2 and was verified on a
fresh installation (run kt30). It adds Java and NetRexx on CMS and in
the Debian guest, and fixes two CP problems with large virtual
machines (I-289, I-290).
:p.The work in progress is the CP guest LAN (milestone 9.12b): a
virtual LAN adapter that CP simulates for each guest, so that Linux
guests can talk to each other without real devices. One guest already
runs on it; the first exchange between two guests found a CP failure
(I-292) that is being worked on.
:h1.The Control Program
:h2.The conversion (M0-M3)
:p.CP is assembled from the CE source with update decks (the XA00nnDK
series) by CE's own assembler, extended for the ESA/390 instructions.
The build runs on the PC under Hercules, stages the object decks, and
writes a nucleus to VM50-1. Every module that touched an S/370 facility
was converted:
:ul.
:li.I/O through the channel subsystem: STSCH, MSCH, SSCH, TSCH, HSCH
and CSCH replace SIO, TIO and HIO. CP's real I/O supervisor (DMKIOS)
was rewritten around subchannels.
:li.Dynamic address translation with ESA/390 segment and page tables.
CP runs with DAT on, in AMODE 31.
:li.ESA/390 PSWs, control registers and interruption codes; the
storage key instructions ISKE, SSKE and IVSK.
:li.IPL of CP from DASD (DMKSAV, DMKCKP, DMKCPI), checkpoint and warm
start.
:eul.
:p.S/370 virtual machines run as before: CMS, the CE service machines
and S/370 guests see the machine VM/370 always showed them.
:h2.Real storage (M4)
:p.CP probes real storage in AMODE 31 at IPL and builds its frame table
from the real size. Real frames up to 128 MB hold guest pages; storage
above them, up to 2 GB, is CP's paging store, reached with MVCL. CP's
own nucleus and free storage stay below 16 MB. The paging I/O, the
channel program translation and the diagnose interfaces use IDAW lists
for frames above 16 MB.
:p.Build 16 fixed the last known limit here (I-290): page slot numbers
are halfwords that CP loads as signed, so the paging store now ends at
32,767 slots and frames end at 128 MB. A user that pages more than 128
MB no longer stops CP with PGS001 or PTR018.
:h2.Virtual machines
:p.CP offers three kinds of virtual machine:
:dl tsize=14.
:dt.S/370
:dd.The VM/370 virtual machine: BC or EC mode, 24-bit, up to 16 MB.
:dt.EC mode, 31-bit
:dd.A System/370 EC-mode machine with 31-bit addressing and storage up
to 256 MB (DEFINE STORAGE; the directory sets each user's maximum).
The EC-mode CMS runs here.
:dt.ESA/390
:dd.A machine with the ESA/390 architecture: channel subsystem
instructions, ESA/390 DAT, SIGP, the timers and DIAGNOSE codes Linux
uses. SET ESA ON takes effect at the next device IPL. Linux/390 runs
here.
:edl.
:p.For ESA/390 machines CP simulates the channel subsystem (DMKVCS),
including channel programs of any length, suspend and resume, and
format-1 CCWs above 16 MB. It shadows the guest's segment and page
tables (DMKVAX). Several ESA/390 users can run at the same time; their
work fields are kept per user.
:h2.Recent CP changes
:ul.
:li.I-288 (build 15): a Debian guest keeps running when its terminal
goes away; it is disconnected, as under z/VM.
:li.I-289 (build 16): programs loaded above 16 MB by the XL loader get
their storage and file names right.
:li.I-290 (build 16): frames and paging store sized so that slot
numbers never go negative.
:li.Guest LAN (not yet in a kit): CP simulates a 3088 model 60 LCS
device for ESA/390 users (CP DEFINE LCS); see Chapter 6.
:eul.
:h1.CMS
:h2.Two CMS systems
:p.The kit has two CMS systems, both built from source by the project
pipeline:
:dl tsize=14.
:dt.IPL CMS
:dd.CE's CMS, unchanged in function: S/370 BC mode, 24-bit, the
classic 800-byte minidisk format.
:dt.IPL 290
:dd.The EC-mode CMS. Programs may run in AMODE 31 and get storage
above 16 MB (HIGHSTOR, CMSSTOR). It reads and writes both the classic
and the EDF disk formats.
:edl.
:h2.Large minidisks (M6)
:p.Under IPL 290, FORMAT with BLKSIZE 4096 writes an EDF disk: a label,
a directory and an allocation map in 4 KB blocks. A full 3380 (885
cylinders, 518 MB) is one minidisk. A file may have any number of
records; 1,400,000 records in one 112 MB file were written and checked.
ACCESS, LISTFILE, STATE, TYPE, COPYFILE, ERASE, RENAME, DISK DUMP and
LOAD, MOVEFILE and COMPARE work on both formats.
:h2.Tools added to CMS
:ul.
:li.TDISK: a temporary disk defined, formatted and accessed in one
command.
:li.SET/QUERY MACHINE, FULIST, GLOBALV, the CP and CMS command tables.
:li.TELL, LXCALL, PHONE, MQTT, RXPIP, TROFF, BOOKIE, LATEX and PSPRINT
on MAINT 19D, so they work under the usual IPL CMS.
:li.The language disk VMLANG (MAINT 19F, LANG EXEC) with every CMS
language of the project.
:eul.
:h1.Linux Guests
:p.Linux 4.0, built for 31-bit ESA/390 with GCC 13, runs in an ESA/390
virtual machine. Debian 7.11 (wheezy, s390) is installed on a 3390-9
disk (LNX190, 6.8 GB, 5.8 GB free).
:ul.
:li.MAINT starts Debian with CP IPL LINUX from its disk (vmzipl writes
the IPL records), or with the LINUX EXEC from the reader. Other users
get their own copy of the disk.
:li.Each user's IP address follows his CTC pair: 0600/0601 is
10.1.1.2, 0602/0603 is 10.1.2.2. SSH works over the CTC link.
:li.Linux and CMS users run side by side; two Debians run at once
(guest pages in frames above 16 MB).
:li.Debian has PostgreSQL 16, mosquitto, Asterisk 1.8, groff, TeX
Live, Ghostscript, Java and NetRexx, and the vmspool daemon that
serves CMS through the spool bridge.
:li.Give Linux enough storage: 64 MB for ordinary work, 192 MB for
Java. MAINT may define up to 256 MB.
:eul.
:note.Two Debians at once work but are slow. The guest LAN work (9.12b)
uses a small ramdisk Linux as the second guest for its tests.
:h1.Languages and Tools
:p.Programs on CMS are built in three ways: natively with CE's
compilers, cross-built on the PC with GCC 13 (-m31) against newlib and
the project's CMS runtime (cmsrt), or natively with the GCC 13 compiler
that itself runs on CMS. Large programs are loaded above 16 MB by the
XL loader.
