:figcap.Languages
:efig.
:p.Utilities: Info-ZIP zip 3.0 and unzip 6.0, gzip, TERSE/UNTERSE
(PACK and SPACK), MQ and MQTT clients, the CHATBOT service machine.
:p.Java on CMS needs IPL 290 and 128 MB of storage. Its threads are
green threads inside the one CMS task, and sockets are not available
to Java programs on CMS.
:h1.Networking and Services
:h2.What works
:ul.
:li.:hp2.CTC links:ehp2. between each Linux guest and the PC
(Hercules CTCI), with per-user addresses (9.12a).
:li.:hp2.The spool bridge:ehp2. (9.25): CMS users call Linux services
through reader and punch. The vmspoold daemon in Debian runs the
services; LXCALL is the general client on CMS.
:li.:hp2.Sockets for CMS:ehp2. (9.5, 9.13d): NICOF, with the CMSFTPD
FTP server, NHFS file transfer and HTTPD, under IPL CMS and IPL 290.
:li.:hp2.Messaging:ehp2. (9.7, 9.14, 9.32): MQ queues on RabbitMQ, MQTT
on mosquitto, TELL between CMS users, Linux users and users on the
internet.
:eul.
:h2.The guest LAN (9.12b, in progress)
:p.The target is one CP-simulated LAN segment for all guests, like the
z/VM guest LAN. Each Linux guest defines two virtual devices with CP
DEFINE LCS (a read and a write subchannel of a 3088 model 60) and
Linux's lcs driver drives them as eth0. CP (module DMKVLN) reads the
guest's own channel programs, so the suspend, resume and PCI flags
that Linux uses work without translation; it answers the LCS control
commands and switches Ethernet frames between the members by MAC
address.
:p.Steps:
:ol.
:li.Step A, CP DEFINE LCS and SENSE ID as a 3088 model 60: done on 14
October; Linux binds its lcs driver to the pair. (A dedicated Hercules
LCS was tried first and cannot run under CP's channel program
translation, I-291; that is why the LAN is simulated.)
:li.Step B, one guest on the LAN: done on 14 October. Debian with
kernel #11 brings up eth0 through CP's LCS: STARTUP, STARTLAN and
LANSTAT are answered, the MAC address is 02 followed by the VMBLOK
and device, and frames are sent.
:li.Step C, two guests ping each other: in progress. The second guest
(CMSUSER, a ramdisk Linux booted from the reader) joins the LAN; the
first frame between the two guests stops CP (I-292).
:li.Step D, cleanup at DETACH and LOGOFF, halt and clear, recovery:
planned.
:eol.
:p.After the LAN come the BORDER firewall service machine (9.12c), the
only link to the outside with NAT and port forwards, and DHCP and DNS
for the inside segment (9.12d).
:h1.Documents and Printing
:p.CMS users format documents in the Debian guest through the spool
bridge:
:dl tsize=12.
:dt.TROFF
:dd.groff with the ms, me, mm and mom macros: text or PostScript.
:dt.BOOKIE
:dd.BookMaster markup, formatted by bookie, the project's own
BookMaster-compatible formatter, in the style of the VM/370plus Guide.
This book is formatted by it.
:dt.LATEX
:dd.TeX Live 2023: latex, pdflatex, dvips.
:dt.PSPRINT
:dd.PostScript to a printer: by the spool (printer 00E to a file on the
PC) or through Ghostscript in Debian.
:edl.
:p.Ghostscript 10.02 in Debian converts PostScript to PDF and to
printer formats. The VM/370plus Guide is produced both as a Word file
on the PC and as GUIDE SCRIPT for BOOKIE on the system itself.
:h1.The Kit
:p.Kit build 16 (14 October 2026) is an overlay for a fresh VM/370 CE
V1 R1.2. Unzip CE, unzip all 18 parts over it, join the Debian disk
(JOINDEB) and the language disk (JOINLANG), and start CE as usual. The
system warm-starts by itself.
